 "capsule": "After crusaders sacked Constantinople in 1204, Theodore I Laskaris gathered Byzantine refugees in Bithynia and, from 1208, ruled as emperor from Nicaea, the strongest of the Greek successor states, which also included Epirus and Trebizond. Under Theodore I, John III Vatatzes, and Theodore II Laskaris, Nicaea rebuilt Byzantine institutions, defeated Latin and rival Greek forces, and steadily recovered territory in Anatolia and Europe. In 1261 the forces of Michael VIII Palaiologos, who had risen from regent for the young John IV Laskaris to co-emperor, recaptured Constantinople, ending Nicaea's exile status and restoring the Byzantine imperial seat under a new Palaiologan dynasty.",
